{"Offsets are marked complete in the `finally` block, which means they're committed even when `result_processor` raises an exception. This implements at-most-once delivery semantics for failed messages (they won't be retried). If this is intentional, it should be documented in the class docstring. If you want at-least-once semantics, move `complete_offset` outside the `finally` block so it only runs on successful processing.": 0.7527737021446228, "Using unbounded `queue.Queue()` instances can lead to unbounded memory growth if workers can't keep up with incoming messages. Consider adding a `maxsize` parameter with appropriate backpressure handling (blocking on submit or dropping messages with metrics) to prevent OOM scenarios under sustained load.": 0.755462646484375, "The commit loop catches all exceptions broadly without any backoff or circuit breaker. If commits persistently fail (e.g., Kafka broker issues), this will create a tight error loop logging repeatedly. Consider adding exponential backoff or a health signal that stops the consumer when commits fail repeatedly.": 0.37874850630760193, "The `queue_pool` is also shut down in `SimpleQueueProcessingStrategy.close()` and `terminate()`. This dual ownership can lead to double-shutdown calls or race conditions. Consider having a single owner - either the factory manages the pool lifecycle and passes it to strategies, or strategies own it exclusively and factory only creates it.": 0.6929224729537964}